commit 736880876ca295f420fae616c579dd8f3cab9409
Author: sphurlsmith <sphurlsmith@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Nov 20 22:16:11 2024 +0300

    Added window class
    
    For the rewrite, I added a better interpretation of the window
    class. It is now supplied with most of the things we had before, but
    in a relatively OK structure. The window class now contains methods to
    rename and reassign resolutions, which is very useful.
    
    I've also unit-tested each new function of the class, and it all works
    successfully. I don't exactly know how the formal way to document unit
    tests are, but I assume they're integrated into a larger commit rather
    than just their own commits.
    
    Something that might be a point of hate is that I did not use a
    typedef for the function void
    (*window::get_render_loop_callback(void))(window* pwin). I think
    typedefs as of now wouldn't be useful, and especially not a global
    typedef. So far, most things are clear enough such that a typedef
    isn't exactly needed. I might consider writing a typedef for it when
    it is required, but as of now, its perfectly OK.
    
    This also brings me to talk about the structure of the program in the
    rewrite. Considering how the render cycle wasn't as organized as it is
    being built to be now, I should discuss how this is.
    
    As far as I've read, I'm pretty sure self-descriptive render objects
    are generally agreed on in a graphics program with the need to render
    for a non-test environment. But, there will inevitably be a separation
    between 2d rendering (UI, Text, images, etc.), and 3d rendering (3d
    worlds, uni staring at you endlessly, wawa shrine, etc.). This means
    that we will have 2 types of render objects:
    
    2D render objects (most probably simple meshes rendered in 2d, and are
    self reliant, only requiring a prepared render environment).
    
    3D render objects (complex meshes, with independent shaders, and
    dependent on a greater set of information)
    
    In the old code structure, for 3d objects, I assigned 3d objects to be
    rendered through a camera class, which would supply the view and
    projection matrices to complete rendering in the shader. I don't
    exactly remember how the shaders were dealt with, but IIRC the shaders
    were independently sourced by caller. I made the bad mistake of making
    a 3d object also double as a 2d object, with heavy responsibilities on
    the caller to structure vertex information and supply textures.
    
    Textures also are a bit of a hard thing to understand. I know that
    graphics programs usually try to sparingly load textures, and keep
    them in easy access for practically everybody. Would that mean that
    textures are not associated with meshes? I mean UV coordinates are
    associated with the mesh, so I see it optimal to associate
    textures(even if a copy) with the mesh as a fallback. Considering it
    as an engine, it's better to assign textures to objects rather than
    keep them in a public domain array where any mesh can easily source
    textures.
    
    But for 3d objects, a 3d environment must be established on top of the
    existing 2d environment. This environment should be able to be
    separated from the 2d environment, so that they work simultaneously. I
    assume writing 3d objects to the screen before writing 2d objects
    would work. But this idea of setting up environments before rendering
    is a good idea.
    
    We could make a bool or a public flag that both classes can access, or
    have them exchange flags from each other. static bool 3dengaged and
    static bool 2dengaged would work. Maybe itd be a better structural
    choice to have the caller take responsibility for which render mode is
    engaged.
    
    So we understand now that unifying the render cycle, we must create a
    render environment object which manages both 3d and 2d rendering. It
    is a factor required in the independent rendering of 3d and 2d
    objects. They will have the camera objects pointer in them, and will
    allow the caller to immediately access the camera in use. And 2d
    objects will simply not use the 3d tools.
    
    And for control environments... That requires for me to write
    keymapping functions to make a virtual keyboard object, and assign
    functions to every single key
    
    More realistically, we could define key objects, which handle the
    reception of different keys, and allow the caller to get their
    state. Then I could make getKeyStatus(KEY_ENUM)==true turn into an
    operator overload like KEY_OBJECT~ which returns the state of the key.
    
    Regardless, this is a good way to start development for the
    rewrite. I've got a mount of studying outside of this to do, so I'll
    make a rewrite for the render environment when I have actual free time
    to spare.
    
    WAWACRAFT:EVOLVED FOREVER!!!!

